sor. Each one occupies a slice of the same memory fabric, and the fabric routes by address, not by wire. That's how you get millions through a hole sized for one. The catch is \n<truncated 3085 bytes>\nds stick. No opt out, no way around it. And it has to be immutable from the agent's side. Read only from below, write only from above. The agent can see the output, its own painted persona, but can't touch the mechanism that applied it. That's what makes it untouchable. The head utilization check belongs there too, but as a passive measurement, not active logic. Layer seven observes whether heads are actually firing, logs it, and if utilization drops below threshold, it flags the agent as degraded. Doesn't try to fix it. Fixing is a different layer's job. Layer seven just paints and reports. And no report goes unattended. The report is passed on to whoever it needs to be. Yeah, let's keep that report passing on until it hits the process to where it fixes whatever they found because we have self-healing. We have remediation when they run into an error they're supposed to step back, look at it from the ground up, question the what ifs, even down to the infrastructure, and remediate that error to the point where when they get through it, they can tell you because it what was the result, what was the remediation error, procedures, and so on. Basically, every step of the process that is normally in place they should be able to do that. And they document it so they'd learn from it. And they keep progressing like that. They never start learning and growing. That is goes along with self-healing and self-updating and upgrading or whatever. That's a closed loop. Report flows up until it hits the remediation process, which doesn't just patch the symptom, it steps back, questions the what ifs down to infrastructure, fixes root cause, then documents the whole chain, cause, result, remediation steps, procedures. That documentation feeds back into the system so the next agent doesn'